sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch11-22> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Spiral shell forms appear with little relation to the kind of animal producing them, recurring in gastropods, cephalopods, foraminifera and tube worms, and mathematically identical forms recur in every geological period and are found mixed together regardless of climate or local conditions.";
    dc:type "empirical-generalization";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "The same spiral shell forms recur across unrelated groups, all ages and all seas";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter XI, “The Logarithmic Spiral”; topic “Shell forms and Natural Selection”. Scope: spiral shells of molluscs, foraminifera and tube-dwelling worms through geological time"
}
